VERDICT: UPHOLD
CHECKER: judge-claude
FAMILY: anthropic
TASK: S4
ATTEMPT: 3
---
LENS: correctness. Ruled against REVIEW_AUG20_SPEC.md's S4 row and ACCESSIBILITY.md
points 10 and 14 as written. I did not read the other two judges' verdicts.

WHAT I VERIFIED MYSELF (real browser, not the checker's harness, not template
reading). Built cmd/server to scratchpad, ran it against an isolated data dir
outside the repo (BUDGET_DATA_DIR=.../scratchpad/jc/data, :18477) with two
accounts (a-wide pattern "usaa", b-narrow pattern "usaa-checking") and a real
usaa-checking-2026.csv on disk, so accounts.OverlapWarnings fires through the
production code path. Drove Chromium via Playwright 1.62.1: keyboard-only
dismissal (page.focus + keyboard.press('Enter'), never .click), real HTMX form
submits for the mutations, MutationObserver installed via addInitScript BEFORE
any page script ran, and CDP Accessibility.getFullAXTree for the a11y tree. The
only file I wrote inside the repo is this verdict; `git status --short` is
unchanged apart from the other judges' verdict files, which I did not open.

BOTH CONTESTED SEQUENCES REPRODUCE IN A REAL BROWSER.

Sequence 1 (dismiss -> overlap cleared by a real HTMX edit -> the same overlap
restored by a real HTMX edit), observed:
  [1 fresh load]        banner=true  dismissBtn=true  key=59318409db522b3d stored=null
                        region="Pattern overlap warning: file \"usaa-checking-2026.csv\" ..."
  [2 dismissed]         mut=1 banner=false dismissBtn=false stored=59318409db522b3d
                        region="Account overlap warning dismissed."
  [3 overlap cleared]   mut=1 banner=false key=""  stored=59318409db522b3d  region=""
  [4 same overlap back] mut=1 banner=false dismissBtn=false key=59318409db522b3d
                        region="Pattern overlap warning: file \"usaa-checking-2026.csv\" ..."
Step 4 is a genuine post-load aria-live mutation (mut=1, region role="status"
aria-live="polite", registered long before) carrying an active-warning
assertion, while the same syncWarnings() call removes the banner. Screen-reader
user hears an active pattern-overlap warning; sighted user sees nothing; nobody
has a dismiss control.

Sequence 2 (dismiss -> ordinary reload, warnings unchanged), observed:
  final region text = "Pattern overlap warning: file \"usaa-checking-2026.csv\" ..."
  banner present = false ; dismiss button in DOM = false
  computed style of region: display block, visibility visible, clip rect(0,0,0,0),
    1x1, aria-hidden null  -> sr-only, i.e. exposed, not display:none
  CDP getFullAXTree after dismiss+reload: 2 nodes containing "Pattern overlap
    warning" (role StaticText and InlineTextBox), ignored=false
So after the dismissal the warning text is live in the accessibility tree on
every subsequent load in the session, with no visible counterpart anywhere and
no control to dismiss it again.

WHERE checker-second IS WRONG ON MECHANISM (and why it does not save the work).
Its Finding 2 claims the seed makes text === lastWarningsText so "the region is
left untouched". That is false. The server renders the region's content
whitespace-padded:
  accounts.html:169-171 ->  "\n        Pattern overlap warning: ...\n    "
while #accounts-warnings-data's data-warnings-text is unpadded. I confirmed this
against the raw served bytes (curl) and against the DOM. Consequence, observed
with a document-wide MutationObserver armed before the page's own script:
EVERY full page load produces two childList mutations on the region -- the
server's padded text, then the script's trimmed rewrite -- on LOAD 1, LOAD 2
(post-dismissal, nothing changed) and LOAD 3 alike. The same-text guard, which
the template's own comment offers as its ACCESSIBILITY.md point 14 mechanism
("assigning identical text on every swap would still count as a DOM mutation to
a screen reader's live-region observer, which is exactly the 're-announced ...
every page load' ACCESSIBILITY.md point 14 rules out"), never holds across a
page load at all. Both mutations occur at readyState==="loading", so most AT
will not speak them; I therefore do not rest the ruling on "it is spoken on
every load". I rest it on the state that persists, which I measured directly in
the accessibility tree. checker-second's harness misdescribed the path and got
the observable end state exactly right; the error understates the problem.

THE WRITTEN TEXT I AM RULING AGAINST.
ACCESSIBILITY.md point 14: "Banners (unassigned files, staleness warnings) are
dismissible via keyboard and re-announced only on state change, not every page
load within a session."
The implementation honours that in the visual channel only. The dismissal
suppresses the amber banner and, with it, the dismiss button; it does nothing to
the sr-only region, which the server re-renders with the full warning text on
every load because the dismissal exists only in the browser. A screen-reader
user who dismisses and reloads gets the warning back in reading order, for the
rest of the session, with the control that would let them dismiss it again now
absent from the DOM. Reading point 14 as satisfied by suppressing only the
sighted channel would make, inside an accessibility standard, a dismissal
requirement that accessibility users cannot obtain -- not a plausible reading of
a document whose neighbouring points (8, 12, 13) exist precisely to demand
parity of meaning across presentation channels. Point 14 is not met.

The S4 row of REVIEW_AUG20_SPEC.md requires "The rendered warning is announced
per ACCESSIBILITY.md". In sequence 1 the warning is announced but not rendered;
in sequence 2 it is rendered only to AT and only after being dismissed. Neither
is "announced per ACCESSIBILITY.md".

ROOT CAUSE, AND WHY THIS IS A DEFECT AND NOT MY TASTE IN FRONT-END DESIGN.
accounts.html documents behaviour the code does not have. The data carrier's
comment says it is emitted even when empty "so a mutation that resolves the last
overlap can clear the live region and the dismissal key too." It does not clear
the dismissal key: syncWarnings() hits `var banner = ...; if (!banner) return;`
and returns before touching sessionStorage. Measured at step 3 above:
data-warnings-key="" while sessionStorage still holds 59318409db522b3d. That
stale key is exactly what suppresses the banner at step 4. The banner block's
comment likewise asserts "a changed warning set is a state change and
reappears/re-announces" -- at step 4 the set changed twice and only the
re-announce half happened. The remedy is small and local (clear the stored key
when data-warnings-key is empty; and either gate the server-rendered region
content on a dismissal the server can see, or clear/normalise the region so the
dismissed state survives a load). A one-line-class fix that restores the
implementation's own documented contract is a defect, not an architectural
preference, and I am not ruling on architecture.

ON POINT 14's "STATE CHANGE" QUESTION PUT TO ME. In sequence 1 the state did
change twice, so re-announcing is not itself the violation -- the pair is
jointly wrong. Point 14's subject is the banner: it is the banner that is
"dismissible" and the banner that is "re-announced ... on state change". At step
4 something is re-announced that is not a banner, because no banner exists and
none can be summoned. The announcement and the presentation are supposed to be
the same event; the diff makes them two independently-keyed pieces of state and
step 4 is the visible cost.

ON THE TWO PASSES. Absence of a finding is not evidence, but one of them is
worse than silent. checker-a11y's point 14 PASS rests on the stated claim that
the dismissal is "purely a visual/announcement-state suppression"; it verified
data-warnings-text after the reload and never the region's own content after the
reload. LOAD 2 above falsifies that claim on the announcement half. checker-tests
scoped itself to the twelve written criteria and explicitly recorded two
uncharged defects (D1, D2), noting D1 "is the same class of problem attempt 2
was failed for" -- which is consistent with, not contrary to, this FAIL.

WHAT WOULD HAVE CHANGED MY MIND. If the sr-only region had been empty (or
carrying the dismissal confirmation) after dismiss+reload, sequence 2 would
collapse and sequence 1 alone would be a thin, arguable basis and I would have
overruled. It is not empty: two un-ignored StaticText/InlineTextBox nodes in
Chromium's accessibility tree say otherwise.

VERDICT: UPHOLD. The FAIL stands. I note for the lead only, without letting it
weigh on the merits, that under CLAUDE.md's hard stop this is S4's third
attempt.
